<!-- 1 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-why">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">01</span><h2>Total vs. Differential Settlement</h2></div>

  <p class="lead">Every foundation settles. That is not a defect &mdash; soil is compressible, and loading it squeezes it. What matters is <em>how much</em>, and far more importantly, <strong>how unevenly</strong>.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <span class="an-icon">&#127983;</span>
    <div>
      <strong>The Leaning Tower of Pisa settled about 3 m and is world-famous.</strong> Mexico City's Palacio de Bellas Artes settled roughly 4 m and is merely an inconvenience &mdash; you step <em>down</em> into it. The difference isn't the amount. Pisa's south side settled more than its north side, and that <em>difference</em> is what tilted it. Uniform settlement moves a building; differential settlement breaks it.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal"><span class="vtag">Total settlement</span><h4>S<sub>t</sub></h4><p>How far the whole foundation goes down. Matters for utility connections, drainage, and access &mdash; a pipe entering the building at a fixed elevation does not settle with it.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-rust"><span class="vtag">Differential settlement</span><h4>&delta;S</h4><p>The difference between two points. This is what cracks walls, jams doors, and tilts structures. Usually the governing criterion.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb"><span class="vtag">Angular distortion</span><h4>&delta;S / L</h4><p>Differential settlement divided by the distance between the points. Roughly 1/500 risks cracking in load-bearing walls; 1/150 risks structural damage.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Settlement, not bearing capacity, governs the design of most shallow foundations on clay. You will often find a footing that is enormous compared to what strength alone requires &mdash; sized entirely to keep settlement tolerable.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 2 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-three">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">02</span><h2>The Three Components</h2></div>

  <p>Settlement arrives in three instalments, on three completely different timescales, driven by three different mechanisms.</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">1 &mdash; Immediate, S<sub>e</sub></span>
      <h4>Elastic distortion (minutes)</h4>
      <p>The soil skeleton deforms in shape without changing volume, like squashing a rubber block. Happens as fast as you apply the load. Dominant in sand and unsaturated soil.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">2 &mdash; Primary, S<sub>c</sub></span>
      <h4>Consolidation (months to years)</h4>
      <p>Water is squeezed out of saturated clay pores and the soil actually loses volume. Slow, because water has to physically travel out. Usually the largest component in clay.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-vio">
      <span class="vtag">3 &mdash; Secondary, S<sub>s</sub></span>
      <h4>Creep (years to decades)</h4>
      <p>The soil skeleton keeps slowly rearranging at constant effective stress, long after pore pressure has dissipated. Matters in organic soils and peat.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Core Idea</span>
    <p>\( S_t = S_e + S_c + S_s \). In sand, S<sub>e</sub> is essentially everything and it is over before construction finishes. In clay, S<sub>c</sub> dominates and can still be developing a decade later.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 3 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-elastic">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">03</span><h2>Immediate (Elastic) Settlement</h2></div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-vio">
      <span class="vtag">Elastic settlement of a flexible foundation</span>
      <div class="eq">\[ S_e = q_o B\,\frac&#123;1-\mu_s^2&#125;&#123;E_s&#125;\,I_s I_f \]</div>
      <p class="small">q<sub>o</sub> = net applied pressure, B = width, &mu;<sub>s</sub> = Poisson's ratio, E<sub>s</sub> = soil modulus, I<sub>s</sub> = shape factor, I<sub>f</sub> = depth factor.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-blu">
      <span class="vtag">Rigid vs. flexible</span>
      <p>A flexible foundation (a thin mat, an embankment) settles more at the centre than the edges &mdash; it dishes. A rigid foundation cannot dish, so it settles uniformly at roughly <strong>93% of the flexible centre value</strong>. Real footings are close to rigid.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>The hard part is E<sub>s</sub>, not the formula.</strong> Soil modulus is not a constant &mdash; it depends on stress level, strain level and drainage. It is usually estimated from SPT or CPT correlations, so a settlement prediction is rarely better than the site investigation behind it.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 4 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-consol">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">04</span><h2>How Clay Consolidates</h2></div>

  <div class="analogy">
    <span class="an-icon">&#129517;</span>
    <div>
      <strong>A saturated clay is a sponge full of water inside a piston.</strong> Push on the piston and, at first, the water takes the entire load &mdash; it cannot escape instantly, and water is nearly incompressible. Over time water leaks out through a small valve; as it leaves, load transfers from the water to the sponge's own fibres, and the sponge compresses. That handover from water to skeleton <em>is</em> consolidation.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">The governing principle</span>
      <h4>Effective stress does the work</h4>
      <p>\( \sigma = \sigma' + u \). The total stress jumps instantly when you load. At t = 0 the pore pressure u carries all of it. As u dissipates, &sigma;' rises by exactly the same amount &mdash; and it is &sigma;' that compresses the soil. Settlement tracks the transfer, not the load.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">What we measure</span>
      <h4>The e&ndash;log&sigma;' curve</h4>
      <p>An oedometer test loads a clay sample in stages and plots void ratio against log of effective stress. It gives a flat <strong>recompression</strong> line of slope C<sub>s</sub> and a steep <strong>virgin compression</strong> line of slope C<sub>c</sub>, meeting at the preconsolidation pressure &sigma;'<sub>c</sub>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="diagram-card">
    <h4>The e&ndash;log&sigma;' Curve</h4>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 330 200" class="static-svg" style="max-width:330px">
      <line x1="45" y1="25" x2="45" y2="165" stroke="var(--bdr2)" stroke-width="1.2"/>
      <line x1="45" y1="165" x2="310" y2="165" stroke="var(--bdr2)" stroke-width="1.2"/>
      <path d="M 60 55 L 165 78" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
      <path d="M 165 78 L 295 148" fill="none" stroke="var(--amb)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
      <line x1="165" y1="78" x2="165" y2="165" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,2"/>
      <circle cx="165" cy="78" r="3.5" fill="var(--rust)"/>
      <text x="168" y="176" font-size="9" fill="var(--rust)" text-anchor="middle">σ'c</text>
      <text x="72" y="48" font-size="9" fill="var(--teal)">recompression, slope Cs</text>
      <text x="215" y="122" font-size="9" fill="var(--amb)">virgin, slope Cc</text>
      <text x="20" y="95" font-size="9" fill="var(--txt2)" transform="rotate(-90 20 95)" text-anchor="middle">void ratio e</text>
      <text x="180" y="192" font-size="9" fill="var(--txt2)" text-anchor="middle">log σ' →</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="cap">The soil "remembers" the highest stress it has ever carried. Below &sigma;'<sub>c</sub> it responds stiffly along the flat recompression line; push past &sigma;'<sub>c</sub> and it falls onto the steep virgin line, where settlement is several times larger for the same load increment.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 5 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-nc">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">05</span><h2>Normally vs. Overconsolidated</h2></div>

  <p>The single most important question about a clay before you compute anything: <strong>has it ever carried more stress than it carries today?</strong> The overconsolidation ratio answers it.</p>

  <div class="vcard v-vio" style="margin-bottom:1rem">
    <span class="vtag">Overconsolidation ratio</span>
    <div class="eq">\[ OCR = \frac&#123;\sigma'_c&#125;&#123;\sigma'_0&#125; \]</div>
    <p class="small">OCR = 1 &rarr; normally consolidated (NC): today's stress is the highest ever. OCR &gt; 1 &rarr; overconsolidated (OC): it has been loaded more before, by ice sheets, by soil since eroded, or by desiccation.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">Case 1 &mdash; NC</span>
      <div class="eq">\[ S_c = \frac&#123;C_c H&#125;&#123;1+e_0&#125;\log\frac&#123;\sigma'_0+\Delta\sigma'&#125;&#123;\sigma'_0&#125; \]</div>
      <p class="small">Entirely on the virgin line.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">Case 2 &mdash; OC, stays below &sigma;'<sub>c</sub></span>
      <div class="eq">\[ S_c = \frac&#123;C_s H&#125;&#123;1+e_0&#125;\log\frac&#123;\sigma'_0+\Delta\sigma'&#125;&#123;\sigma'_0&#125; \]</div>
      <p class="small">Entirely on the recompression line &mdash; small.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-rust">
      <span class="vtag">Case 3 &mdash; OC, crosses &sigma;'<sub>c</sub></span>
      <div class="eq">\[ S_c = \frac&#123;C_s H&#125;&#123;1+e_0&#125;\log\frac&#123;\sigma'_c&#125;&#123;\sigma'_0&#125; + \frac&#123;C_c H&#125;&#123;1+e_0&#125;\log\frac&#123;\sigma'_0+\Delta\sigma'&#125;&#123;\sigma'_c&#125; \]</div>
      <p class="small">Two segments: stiff, then soft.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>This is where the money is.</strong> C<sub>s</sub> is typically only 1/5 to 1/10 of C<sub>c</sub>. Worked Examples 1 and 2 run the identical clay layer and load through Cases 1 and 2 and get <strong>110 mm versus 21 mm</strong>. Loading an OC clay while staying below &sigma;'<sub>c</sub> is the cheapest settlement control there is &mdash; and it is why preloading a site before building works.</div>
  </div>
</section>

