<!-- 7 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-time">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">07</span><h2>Time Rate of Consolidation</h2></div>

  <p>Knowing the settlement will eventually be 110 mm is only half the answer. A client needs to know whether that happens in six months or sixty years.</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">Intuition</span>
      <h4>Water has to travel</h4>
      <p>Consolidation is limited by how fast pore water can escape. The further it must travel and the tighter the soil, the slower it goes &mdash; which is why the drainage path, not the layer thickness, is what enters the equation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">The drainage path H<sub>dr</sub></span>
      <h4>The detail everyone gets wrong</h4>
      <p>If the clay layer has sand above <em>and</em> below, water escapes both ways and only travels half the thickness: <strong>H<sub>dr</sub> = H/2</strong>. Drainage on one side only gives H<sub>dr</sub> = H. Since time goes as H<sub>dr</sub><sup>2</sup>, getting this wrong is a factor-of-four error.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-vio">
      <span class="vtag">The Math</span>
      <div class="eq">
        \[ T_v = \frac&#123;c_v t&#125;&#123;H_&#123;dr&#125;^2&#125; \]
        \[ T_v = \frac&#123;\pi&#125;&#123;4&#125;\left(\frac&#123;U\%&#125;&#123;100&#125;\right)^2 \ (U &lt; 60\%) \]
        \[ T_v = 1.781 - 0.933\log(100-U\%) \ (U &gt; 60\%) \]
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Core Idea</span>
    <p>Time scales with the <strong>square</strong> of the drainage path. Halving the distance water must travel cuts the time to a quarter &mdash; which is exactly the principle behind <strong>vertical sand drains</strong> and wick drains: they don't make the soil stronger, they just give water a much shorter route out.</p>
  </div>
</section>

